    {
      "filename": "Belfast City Council Seat Share by Year 1973-2023.png",
      "title": "File:Belfast City Council Seat Share by Year 1973-2023.png",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/40/Belfast_City_Council_Seat_Share_by_Year_1973-2023.png",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belfast_City_Council_Seat_Share_by_Year_1973-2023.png",
      "width": 1556,
      "height": 658,
      "mime": "image/png",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "LQ192",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "Belfast City Council Seat Share by Year 1973-2023",
      "description": "Share of seats to unionist nationalist or other/cross-community parties in the Belfast City Council from 1973 to 2019. Alliance councillors are counted as other in all years.\nUnionist seat share is shown in blue with triangular markers. This line has generally been trending downwards, at a fairly constant rate, since 1973 where it started at 67%. It went below 50% in 1997, and is currently the second largest group at 28%.\nNationalist seat share is shown in green with circular markers. This line was increasing fairly consistently from 1973, where it started at 18%, until reaching 45% in 2001, where it has since plateaued. It's higest share has been 47% in 2011, and is currently the largest group on 46.6%.\n\nThe other/cross-community seat share is shown in green with square markers. This line was in a fairly steady plateau below 16% since 1973, until it has increased rapidly in recent elections, reaching 25% in 2023, making it the third largest group. There was one exception to this graph in 1977, where it gained 27% of the seats, before falling back below 16% for the rest of the century.",
      "dateCreated": "2025-05-17",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "belfast-city-council-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcey"
    },
